---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AssetPlaceholder from '../../components/AssetPlaceholder.astro';
import KairaLogicFlow from '../../components/KairaLogicFlow.astro';

import csHero from '../../assets/cs-hero.png';
import goodflipOverview from '../../assets/cgm-context-goodflip.png';
import kairaAudiences from '../../assets/kaira-audiences.png';
import kairaOldFlow from '../../assets/kaira-old-flow.png';
import kairaOfferingBack from '../../assets/kaira-offering-back.png';
import processCompetitive from '../../assets/process-competitive.png';
import emotionalMap from '../../assets/emotional-map.png';
import insightBlueprint from '../../assets/insight-blueprint.png';
import kairaLogoLandscape from '../../assets/kaira-logo-landscape.png';
import kairaThyroxine from '../../assets/kaira-thyroxine.png';
import kairaGradient from '../../assets/kaira-gradient.png';
import kairaPatternMolecule from '../../assets/kaira-pattern-molecule.png';
import kairaFinal from '../../assets/kaira-final.png';
import kairaIconSamples from '../../assets/kaira-icon-samples.png';

// Full-resolution exports served from public/ for the zoom lightbox.
const LOGIC_FULL = '/assets/logic-full.png';
const INSIGHT_FULL = '/assets/insight-blueprint-full.png';
const OFFERING_FULL = '/assets/offering-back-full.png';

// Interactive prototype of the logging flow (Figma). Session token stripped.
const protoUrl =
  'https://www.figma.com/proto/DJqxPf65KHQ1HJ0S31eYxr/Food-Logging-with-Kaira?node-id=1732-14602&scaling=scale-down&content-scaling=fixed&starting-point-node-id=1732%3A14602&page-id=0%3A1';
const figmaEmbed = `https://www.figma.com/embed?embed_host=share&url=${encodeURIComponent(protoUrl)}`;

const CGM_HREF = '/work/goodflip-cgm';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Turning food logging from a chore into a habit | Daksh Sharma"
  description="Case study: good logging feels like talking, not filling. Replacing a food-logging form with a conversation. Sole product designer, GoodFlip."
>
  <article class="cs">
    <a class="cs__back" href="/#work">← Back to work</a>

    <figure class="cs__hero">
      <Image
        src={csHero}
        alt="Kaira app: a conversational meal log and the resulting insight card with calories, macros, and a meal score, around the Kaira hexagon mark"
        widths={[900, 1440, 2000, 3360]}
        sizes="min(60rem, 92vw)"
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </figure>

    {/* 1. HERO AND TITLE */}
    <header class="cs__anchor">
      <h1 class="cs__title">Turning food logging from a chore into a habit</h1>
      <p class="cs__hook">Good logging feels like talking, not filling. So I stopped designing a form.</p>
      <p class="cs__role">
        Sole product designer, GoodFlip. A metabolic health platform helping thousands of people
        manage chronic conditions.
      </p>
    </header>

    {/* 2. LET'S START WITH SOME CONTEXT */}
    <section class="context">
      <h2 class="context__heading">Let's start with some context</h2>
      <div class="context__grid">
        <div class="context__col">
          <figure class="context__fig">
            <Image class="context__img" src={goodflipOverview} alt="GoodFlip: a metabolic health platform of programs, devices, diagnostics and coaching" widths={[144, 216]} sizes="4.5rem" />
          </figure>
          <h3 class="context__title">What is GoodFlip</h3>
          <p class="context__desc">
            GoodFlip is a metabolic health platform that helps people manage conditions like
            obesity, fatty liver and PCOS through lifestyle change, with personalised coaching,
            doctor led programs, advanced diagnostics, smart devices and natural supplements.
            Almost all of it depends on one thing people rarely track well: what they eat.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="context__col">
          <figure class="context__fig">
            <Image class="context__img" src={kairaAudiences} alt="The two GoodFlip user types: someone in a doctor led program, and someone on their own health journey" widths={[144, 216]} sizes="4.5rem" />
          </figure>
          <h3 class="context__title">Who we build for</h3>
          <p class="context__desc">
            <strong>People managing chronic conditions.</strong> They come in through a doctor led
            program and have a coach. They logged, mostly because someone asked them to.
          </p>
          <p class="context__desc">
            <strong>Fitness enthusiasts and people on their own health journey.</strong> No coach,
            no diagnosis, no one asking. Mostly, they did not log at all.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* 3. HIGHLIGHTS OF THE PROJECT */}
    <section class="beat">
      <h2 class="beat__h">Highlights of the project</h2>
    </section>
    <dl class="highlights3">
      <div class="stat stat--card">
        <dd class="stat__value">~9 in 10</dd>
        <dt class="stat__label">log with Kaira on their own, with no coach or diet plan behind them</dt>
        <dd class="stat__base">Backend export, roughly 900 Kaira users, Jan to Jun 2026</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="stat stat--card">
        <dd class="stat__value">1 in 4</dd>
        <dt class="stat__label">came back to log on 5 or more separate days, unprompted</dt>
        <dd class="stat__base">Same export. Directional, not a standard retention metric</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="stat stat--card">
        <dd class="stat__value">92.5%</dd>
        <dt class="stat__label">positive feedback on Kaira's responses</dt>
        <dd class="stat__base">172 of 186 rated responses</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    {/* 4. MY ROLE IN THE PROJECT */}
    <section class="beat">
      <h2 class="beat__h">My role in the project</h2>
      <p>
        I was the sole designer on Kaira, from research to launch and beyond. I defined the
        design metrics and owned Kaira's CSAT and DSAT scores, ran the research, designed the
        conversation logic and the design system Kaira is built on, and after launch audited
        4,000 conversations to decide what to fix next. That audit became the roadmap I took to
        product and marketing, including a change to the app's information architecture.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* 5. LET'S LOG A MEAL ON GOODFLIP — dark, matching the CGM "Experience" section */}
    <section class="experience">
      <h2 class="experience__title">Let's log a meal on GoodFlip</h2>
      <div class="experience__content">
        <p class="experience__lead">
          You just had a dosa with a cup of sambhar. Was it actually good for you? Log it and
          find out.
        </p>
        <div class="exp-proto">
          <iframe
            src={figmaEmbed}
            title="Interactive Figma prototype of the Kaira food logging flow"
            loading="lazy"
            allowfullscreen
          ></iframe>
        </div>
        <p class="experience__cap">
          Speak, type or snap. Kaira fills in what it can, shows you what it assumed, and logs
          the meal when you confirm.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="cs__divider" />

    {/* 6. WHAT THE APP OFFERED BEFORE */}
    <section class="beat">
      <h2 class="beat__h">What the app offered before</h2>
      <p>
        Before Kaira, logging a meal meant searching for each item, selecting it, setting a
        portion, and repeating that for every dish in every meal. Logging a single item took 5
        taps across <span class="text-ph">NN</span> screens.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* VIZ-01 — old flow vs Kaira, step count */}
    <figure class="viz viz01">
      <div class="viz01__row">
        <span class="viz01__rowlabel">Old flow</span>
        <div class="viz01__strip">
          <span class="viz01__seg">Search</span>
          <span class="viz01__seg">Select</span>
          <span class="viz01__seg">Portion</span>
          <span class="viz01__seg">Confirm</span>
          <span class="viz01__seg viz01__seg--repeat">Repeat per item</span>
        </div>
        <span class="viz01__count">5 taps</span>
      </div>
      <div class="viz01__row">
        <span class="viz01__rowlabel">Kaira</span>
        <div class="viz01__strip viz01__strip--short">
          <span class="viz01__seg viz01__seg--accent">Speak</span>
          <span class="viz01__seg viz01__seg--accent">Confirm</span>
        </div>
        <span class="viz01__count">2 taps</span>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="viz__cap">The step count, old flow against Kaira, drawn to scale.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="shot shot--hero">
      <Image class="shot__img" src={kairaOldFlow} alt="The previous food logging flow: the health tracker home, the diet tab with a day's meal slots, the food search screen, and the manual macro entry form" widths={[900, 1440, 2000]} sizes="min(60rem, 92vw)" />
      <figcaption>
        The previous food logging flow: home, the diet tab, searching for an item, and setting
        the portion and macros by hand.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="beat">
      <p>
        The old flow asked the user for everything. That is the assumption the rest of this
        project takes apart.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* 7. THE PROBLEM STATEMENT */}
    <section class="problem">
      <div class="problem__inner">
        <h2 class="problem__heading">The problem statement</h2>
        <p class="problem__text">
          Nutrition is one of the biggest levers on metabolism, and unlike steps or sleep, no
          device can track it for you. Someone has to log it. At GoodFlip we saw that people did,
          but almost only when a coach asked. Left alone, the effort was not worth it.
        </p>
        <p class="problem__text problem__text--highlight">
          So the challenge was twofold: make logging cost so little that people would do it on
          their own, and give them something back that made it worth doing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="cs__section-break" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="cs__section-break-line"></span>
      <span class="cs__section-break-dot"></span>
      <span class="cs__section-break-line"></span>
    </div>

    {/* 8. WHY THIS PROBLEM MATTERED */}
    <section class="beat">
      <h2 class="beat__h">Why this problem mattered</h2>
    </section>

    <div class="perspective">
      <div class="perspective__col perspective__col--user">
        <h3 class="perspective__heading">Looking at it from the user's side</h3>
        <p class="perspective__intro">
          Today someone making lifestyle changes has to lean on a coach, use a separate app, or
          wing it.
        </p>
        <ol class="perspective__list">
          <li>I do everything for my lifestyle change in one place instead of stitching apps together.</li>
          <li>My coach and doctor see what I actually eat, so my plan fits my real life instead of a guess.</li>
          <li>GoodFlip is clinically led, so what it tells me about my meals carries more weight than a generic calorie app.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="perspective__col perspective__col--business">
        <h3 class="perspective__heading">The business case</h3>
        <ol class="perspective__list">
          <li><strong>A reason to open the app today.</strong> Supplements, programs, devices and diagnostics are all periodic. Food is daily. Without logging there was very little a user could do on any given day.</li>
          <li><strong>A wider top of funnel.</strong> Tracking meals is commonly the first thing people try when they start a health journey, and every competitor already offered it.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    {/* 9. BREAKING DOWN THE PROBLEM */}
    <section class="beat">
      <h2 class="beat__h">Breaking down the problem</h2>
      <p>
        Building a food logging habit is too big to solve in one release. A single all in one
        solution would take months before anyone benefited. So I broke the problem into stages,
        each of which could ship and improve logging on its own.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* VIZ-02 — Awareness, Action, Retention, as a chevron progression */}
    <div class="viz viz02">
      <div class="viz02__callouts">
        <div class="viz02__callout">
          <span class="viz02__tag">Deferred</span>
          <p>Nothing tells people to log. It reads as a collection of services, not a daily habit.</p>
          <span class="viz02__leader" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </div>
        <span class="viz02__spacer" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="viz02__callout">
          <span class="viz02__tag">Deferred</span>
          <p>Understanding what someone's working towards, and showing progress against it.</p>
          <span class="viz02__leader" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="viz02__chevrons">
        <div class="viz02__chevron">Awareness</div>
        <div class="viz02__chevron viz02__chevron--active">Action</div>
        <div class="viz02__chevron">Retention</div>
      </div>

      <div class="viz02__callouts viz02__callouts--bottom">
        <span class="viz02__spacer" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="viz02__callout viz02__callout--hero">
          <span class="viz02__leader viz02__leader--up" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="viz02__tag viz02__tag--active">Phase one</span>
          <p>The steps a person takes to log a meal, and what each step costs them. The one stage
          that improved the product on its own.</p>
        </div>
        <span class="viz02__spacer" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="viz__cap">
      Each stage could ship on its own. I started with the one that improved the product without
      waiting for the others.
    </p>

    <div class="cs__wide">
      <table class="stagetable">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Stage</th><th>What it means</th><th>Status</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="stagetable__name">Awareness</td>
            <td>The app does not tell anyone to log. It reads as a collection of services, not a daily habit. This is an IA and narrative problem.</td>
            <td>Deferred</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="stagetable__name">Action</td>
            <td>The steps a person takes to log a meal, and what each of those steps costs them.</td>
            <td><strong>Phase one</strong></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="stagetable__name">Retention</td>
            <td>Understanding what the user is actually working towards and showing progress against it. Logging alone cannot carry this.</td>
            <td>Deferred</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <section class="beat">
      <p>
        I started with Action, because it was the only stage that improved the product on its
        own. Awareness and Retention come back at the end of this story.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* VIZ-03 — impact vs effort, a proper two-axis quadrant */}
    <figure class="viz viz03">
      <div class="viz03__chart">
        <div class="viz03__ylabel">Impact</div>
        <div class="viz03__plotwrap">
          <div class="viz03__plot">
            <span class="viz03__pt viz03__pt--action" style="left: 27%; top: 24%;">Action</span>
            <span class="viz03__pt" style="left: 30%; top: 75%;">Clean up the form</span>
            <span class="viz03__pt" style="left: 75%; top: 45%;">Awareness</span>
            <span class="viz03__pt" style="left: 80%; top: 20%;">Retention</span>
          </div>
          <div class="viz03__xrow">
            <span class="viz03__tick">Low</span>
            <span class="viz03__xlabel">Effort</span>
            <span class="viz03__tick">High</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="viz__cap">Impact versus effort for the paths I could take. Not an Eisenhower matrix. That is urgent versus important.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="beat">
      <h3 class="beat__sub">The fork</h3>
      <p>With Action scoped, there were two ways forward:</p>
    </section>

    {/* Fork diagram: two methods converging on the one I chose */}
    <figure class="viz forkviz">
      <svg class="forkviz__svg" viewBox="0 0 640 220" role="img" aria-label="Two methods considered: Method 1, clean up the existing flow; Method 2, enable new ways of logging through AI. Method 2 was chosen, leading to the AI led path.">
        <path class="forkviz__path forkviz__path--rejected" d="M260,52 C330,52 410,112 460,112" />
        <path class="forkviz__path forkviz__path--chosen" d="M260,172 C330,172 410,112 460,112" />

        <g>
          <rect x="0" y="20" width="260" height="64" rx="12" class="forkviz__rect" />
          <text x="16" y="44" class="forkviz__tag">Method 1</text>
          <text x="16" y="66" class="forkviz__desc">Clean up the existing flow</text>
        </g>

        <g>
          <rect x="0" y="140" width="260" height="64" rx="12" class="forkviz__rect forkviz__rect--chosen" />
          <text x="16" y="164" class="forkviz__tag forkviz__tag--chosen">Method 2</text>
          <text x="16" y="182" class="forkviz__desc">
            <tspan x="16" dy="0">Enable new ways of logging</tspan>
            <tspan x="16" dy="14">through AI</tspan>
          </text>
        </g>

        <g>
          <rect x="460" y="84" width="180" height="56" rx="28" class="forkviz__pillrect" />
          <text x="550" y="118" text-anchor="middle" class="forkviz__pilltext">AI-led path</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
      <figcaption class="viz__cap">Two ways forward, and the one I chose.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="beat">
      <p>I chose the <span class="pill-inline">AI-led path</span>, and kept manual logging as the fallback.</p>
    </section>

    <ol class="decisions4 reasons4">
      <li class="fix fix--pulled">
        <h4 class="fix__lead">A cleaner form still asks for everything.</h4>
        <p>AI could infer most of what a log needs and ask only for what it could not assume.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="fix">
        <h4 class="fix__lead">People log differently.</h4>
        <p>Some want to type, some to speak, some to snap a photo. AI could meet each of them.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="fix">
        <h4 class="fix__lead">Users already expected it.</h4>
        <p>Competitors had AI logging, so it matched what people were used to instead of teaching them something new.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="fix">
        <h4 class="fix__lead">Richer data for better insights.</h4>
        <p>Natural logs told us more about each person, which fed back into more personal guidance.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <section class="beat">
      <p>What shape that AI should take was still an open question. The research answered it.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="cs__section-break" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="cs__section-break-line"></span>
      <span class="cs__section-break-dot"></span>
      <span class="cs__section-break-line"></span>
    </div>

    {/* 10. THE PROCESS */}
    <section class="beat">
      <h2 class="beat__h">The process</h2>
    </section>

    {/* 10a. Understanding the landscape */}
    <section class="beat">
      <h3 class="beat__sub stepnum">Understanding the landscape</h3>
      <p>
        GoodFlip was not the first app to offer snap, voice and text logging. People arrive
        already carrying habits from other apps, so I started by mapping what they were used to.
        Three things I tracked across every competitor:
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="thought-bubbles">
      <div class="thought-bubble">
        <p>What the app asks you to do the moment you open it</p>
        <span class="thought-bubble__tail" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
      </div>
      <div class="thought-bubble">
        <p>How long it takes to log one meal</p>
        <span class="thought-bubble__tail" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
      </div>
      <div class="thought-bubble">
        <p>What you get back once the meal is logged</p>
        <span class="thought-bubble__tail" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figure class="shot shot--compact">
      <Image class="shot__img" src={processCompetitive} alt="Competitive teardown comparing how logging opens, how long it takes, and what each app gives back" widths={[500, 700]} sizes="26rem" />
      <figcaption>
        I map what already exists and where it falls short, so I design against the real gap
        rather than a blank page.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p class="optional-note">
      Optional: <span class="text-ph">IMG-04: competitor logging time comparison</span>, if the
      numbers from the landscape study are on hand. This connects directly to the effort spine.
    </p>

    {/* 10b. Understanding the people */}
    <section class="beat">
      <h3 class="beat__sub stepnum">Understanding the people</h3>
    </section>

    <div class="fielddoc">
      <span class="fielddoc__tape" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <div class="fielddoc__header">
        <span class="fielddoc__label">Field notes</span>
      </div>

      <p class="fielddoc__intro">
        I did not want logging to feel like an extra task. So before designing anything, I ran a
        short study.
      </p>

      <div class="fielddoc__pills">
        <span class="pill-chip">8 people</span>
        <span class="pill-chip">In the office</span>
        <span class="pill-chip">Different ages and backgrounds</span>
      </div>

      <div class="fielddoc__aim">
        <span class="fielddoc__aimtag">The aim</span>
        <p>
          To see how people naturally talk about what they eat, and where that natural speech
          falls short of what a log actually needs.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="study-conditions">
        <div class="study-cond">
          <span class="study-cond__label">Free, open prompt</span>
          <p class="study-cond__quote">"Describe everything you ate and did yesterday."</p>
          <p class="study-cond__note">People narrated naturally. 12 to 58 seconds.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="study-cond">
          <span class="study-cond__label">Nudged, guided prompt</span>
          <p class="study-cond__quote">"What did you have for dinner yesterday, and how much?"</p>
          <p class="study-cond__note">People answered faster and more precisely. 10 to 20 seconds.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="fielddoc__block">
        <p>I also asked each person four things, listed plainly:</p>
        <ul class="plainlist plainlist--bullet">
          <li>Would you log after each meal, or at the end of the day?</li>
          <li>What is a reasonable amount of time to spend logging a meal?</li>
          <li>Why would you log at all?</li>
          <li>How likely are you to log everything, including the meals you are not proud of?</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="fielddoc__block">
        <p><strong>What I found</strong>, as three short findings:</p>
      </div>

      <ol class="findingshl">
        <li class="findinghl">
          <span class="findinghl__num">1</span>
          <div class="findinghl__body">
            <h4>Free speech is rich but data poor.</h4>
            <p>People narrated their whole day, including things a food log does not need, and left out the things it does: quantity, portion and timing.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="findinghl">
          <span class="findinghl__num">2</span>
          <div class="findinghl__body">
            <h4>A nudge recovers the structure, but too many nudges kill it.</h4>
            <p>One specific question made people faster and more precise. A form's worth of questions would not have.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="findinghl">
          <span class="findinghl__num">3</span>
          <div class="findinghl__body">
            <h4>People log at the end of the day, not meal by meal.</h4>
            <p>This became a hypothesis that shaped the whole conversation design, and the March data later confirmed it.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="honesty-line">
        Eight people in an office is a small sample and not our exact audience. It was enough to
        form a hypothesis, not to prove one.
      </p>

      {/* VIZ-04 — free vs nudged timing */}
      <figure class="viz viz04">
      <div class="viz04__row">
        <span class="viz04__label">Free speech</span>
        <div class="viz04__track">
          <div class="viz04__bar" style="left: 12%; width: 58%;">
            <span class="viz04__end">12s</span>
            <span class="viz04__end viz04__end--right">58s</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="viz04__row">
        <span class="viz04__label">Nudged</span>
        <div class="viz04__track">
          <div class="viz04__bar viz04__bar--accent" style="left: 10%; width: 10%;">
            <span class="viz04__end">10s</span>
            <span class="viz04__end viz04__end--right">20s</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="viz04__labels">
        <span>natural but data poor</span>
        <span>precise but rigid if you ask too much</span>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="viz__cap">Time to describe yesterday's meals, free speech against a single nudge. Same people, same task, two prompts.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    {/* 10c. Mapping how people feel when they log */}
    <section class="beat">
      <h3 class="beat__sub stepnum">Mapping how people feel when they log</h3>
      <p>
        People arrive at the food log from different places and in very different states.
        Someone who just saw a glucose spike is curious. Someone on day forty of a program is
        bored. Someone who broke their streak feels guilty. So I mapped the emotional path rather
        than only the functional one, following the CGM triggered route into logging: curious
        when the reading appears, motivated, then bored once it turns routine, anxious at a bad
        number, guilty after a missed day.
      </p>
    </section>

    <figure class="shot shot--hero">
      <Image class="shot__img" src={emotionalMap} alt="Emotional journey map: stages, user thoughts, emotions, and app touchpoints across the CGM-triggered path into logging" widths={[900, 1440, 2000]} sizes="min(60rem, 92vw)" />
      <figcaption>
        The emotional journey I mapped for the CGM triggered path into logging. This told me the
        flow had to meet people where they were, not just capture their data.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    {/* 10d. Deciding on a chat experience */}
    <section class="beat">
      <h3 class="beat__sub stepnum">Deciding on a chat experience</h3>
      <p>
        The study and the landscape together made one thing clear: we needed snap, voice and
        text. This would also be the first major AI feature GoodFlip put in front of the public.
        That could go two ways.
      </p>
      <ol class="plainlist">
        <li>Build three separate experiences: one for voice, one for text, one for the camera</li>
        <li>Build one AI surface that all three live inside</li>
      </ol>
      <p>I chose the second.</p>
    </section>

    <ol class="decisions4 reasons3">
      <li class="fix">
        <h4 class="fix__lead">One thing to learn, not three.</h4>
        <p>People log from several places in the app. A single conversation meant every entry point behaved the same way.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="fix">
        <h4 class="fix__lead">It had somewhere to go.</h4>
        <p>We were already planning insights on CGM readings, body composition and more. A single AI surface meant that work compounded instead of ending at food.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="fix">
        <h4 class="fix__lead">A bet on curiosity.</h4>
        <p>I assumed people would want more than a calorie count once they had somewhere to ask. A unified experience made that cheap to find out.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <section class="beat">
      <p>That third reason was a bet. Section 13 is where the data tells me whether it paid off.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="cs__section-break" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="cs__section-break-line"></span>
      <span class="cs__section-break-dot"></span>
      <span class="cs__section-break-line"></span>
    </div>

    {/* 11. EXECUTING PHASE ONE */}
    {/* 11a. Infer, don't interrogate */}
    <section class="beat">
      <p class="display-line stepnum">Infer, don't interrogate</p>
      <p>
        The research said a nudge recovers what free speech leaves out. The obvious fix is to ask
        more questions. That is also the fastest way to make someone quit.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I built the opposite. Let people speak however they naturally do, then infer the
        missing structure instead of demanding it. I went through every field a log needs and
        split it in two: what Kaira must ask, and what she can safely assume. For everything
        assumable I wrote the fallback behind it.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* VIZ-05 — must ask vs can assume, with fallback chain */}
    <div class="viz viz05">
      <div class="viz05__cols">
        <div class="viz05__col viz05__col--ask">
          <h4 class="viz05__colhead">Must ask</h4>
          <ul class="viz05__list">
            <li>Meal type</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="viz05__col viz05__col--assume">
          <h4 class="viz05__colhead">Can assume</h4>
          <ul class="viz05__list">
            <li>Quantity and portion</li>
            <li>Time of day</li>
            <li>Ingredients for common dishes</li>
            <li>Likely repeat meals</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="viz05__chain">
        <span class="viz05__link">Coach plan</span>
        <span class="viz05__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        <span class="viz05__link">User history</span>
        <span class="viz05__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        <span class="viz05__link">Population defaults</span>
        <span class="viz05__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        <span class="viz05__link">Reason from scratch</span>
      </div>
      <p class="viz__cap">
        Kaira works down this chain for every field it does not ask about, and always shows what
        it assumed.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        There was exactly one field I refused to let it assume: meal type. Because people log at
        the end of the day, "poha and chai" could be breakfast or an evening snack, and getting
        that wrong quietly poisons the data. So Kaira always asks that one thing and assumes the
        rest. That single no is the design.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="shot shot--hero">
      <KairaLogicFlow />
      <figcaption>
        The logic I handed to engineering: speak freely, infer what can be inferred, ask gently
        for what cannot, and never dead end on a vague answer. The system is allowed to guess. It
        is never allowed to hide that it guessed.
        <button
          class="expand-link"
          data-lightbox={LOGIC_FULL}
          data-caption="The complete assumption-engine flow: what Kaira asks, what she infers, the food-database lookup, and the GPT fallback for unusual inputs."
        >
          See the full logic I handed to engineering →
        </button>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="beat">
      <p>
        I also designed a flow for logging several meals at once, for the person who dumps their
        whole day in one go. It was cut for dev effort before launch. Remember this one, because
        the data brings it back later.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* 11b. Offering something back */}
    <section class="beat">
      <h3 class="beat__sub stepnum">Offering something back</h3>
      <p>
        Nir Eyal's Hooked model describes a habit as a loop: a trigger, an action, a reward and an
        investment. Three of those we could build. A nudge is a trigger. Everything above made
        the action cheaper. A growing health record is the investment. The hard one is the
        reward, because it has to arrive instantly and it has to be worth the log.
      </p>
      <p>
        Macros and goal progress are easy to show and easy to ignore. I wanted people to
        understand what a meal had actually done to them, so I wrote the response logic myself: a
        summary of the meal, the macro read, the likely sugar spike, and a single Meal Score that
        answered the only question anyone really asks, which is whether that was a good idea.
      </p>
    </section>

    <figure class="shot shot--support">
      <button
        class="zoomable"
        data-lightbox={INSIGHT_FULL}
        data-caption="The response blueprint: calories, macros, spike risk and a metabolic verdict, always ending on the likely sugar spike."
        aria-label="Zoom the meal-level insight logic"
