    <blockquote class="pullquote">
      <p>
        GoodFlip is a complicated platform, with doctors, devices, diagnostics and supplements
        behind it. None of that complexity is the user's problem to carry. My job is to absorb it
        and hand them one clear next step.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    {/* 15. FUTURE OF KAIRA */}
    <section class="beat">
      <h2 class="beat__h">Future of Kaira</h2>
      <p>
        Kaira started as a way to log food. She is becoming the way people ask GoodFlip anything
        about their body.
      </p>
      <p>
        The work in flight gives her more places to speak from, with insights on CGM readings,
        body composition and the rest, and makes those conversations two way, so a person can ask
        why instead of only reading what. Beyond that I have pitched a live Kaira that reads a
        meal through the camera in real time, and capture through wearables like smart glasses,
        so logging eventually stops being a task a person performs at all.
      </p>
      <p class="display-line display-line--small">
        The direction is consistent: every version of Kaira should ask the user for less than the
        last one.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* 16. MY NORTH STAR */}
    <section class="beat">
      <h2 class="beat__h">My north star</h2>
      <p>
        I want food logging on GoodFlip to be the easiest honest record of what a person eats,
        anywhere. Easy enough that someone with no coach and no diagnosis keeps doing it, and
        honest enough that a doctor can build a plan on it.
      </p>
      <p>
        If that holds, GoodFlip stops being an app people force themselves to open and becomes
        the place their health actually lives, with clinical depth behind it that no general
        wellness app can match.
      </p>
      <p>
        We are not there yet. But the path from one logged dosa to a plan built on real data is
        the shortest one in metabolic health, and I would like GoodFlip to own it.
      </p>
